# Full pages for the places on the themed trails that had only a short paragraph (Ifan, 12 November 2026:
# "a page on each of these"). long.py puts LONG into PLACE_LONG, so these replace the short text on place-<id>.html.
# Format as in src/sixth_century.py: "## " starts a heading, "> words | who said it" is a quotation.
# Follow EDITORIAL.md: proud and true, Britons not Celts, nothing negative about the people of Welsh history.

LONG = {
 "paviland": (
  ["Goat’s Hole at Paviland is a sea cave in the limestone cliffs of the south coast of Gower, between Port Eynon and Rhosili. Today the waves reach its mouth at high tide. When people first came here, the sea was far away, and the cave looked out over a wide plain where mammoth, woolly rhinoceros, reindeer and wild horses roamed.",
   "## The Red Lady",
   "In January 1823 the Reverend William Buckland, professor of geology at Oxford, dug in the cave and found a skeleton stained deep red with ochre, laid with rods and rings of mammoth ivory and with periwinkle shells. Because of the ornaments he took it for a woman, and thought it dated from Roman times. The name stuck: the Red Lady of Paviland.",
   "## A young man, 33,000 years ago",
   "Modern science tells a far older story. The bones are those of a young man, perhaps in his twenties, and radiocarbon dating shows that he was buried about 33,000 years ago, in the Old Stone Age, before the last great ice sheets covered Wales. He was laid in the cave with care, his body coloured with red ochre, with ornaments and with the skull of a mammoth nearby.",
   "That makes Paviland the oldest known ceremonial burial in Britain, and one of the oldest in western Europe. Thousands of flint tools found in the cave show that people came back to it again and again over thousands of years, and may have honoured it as a special place.",
   "## Visiting",
   "The bones are kept at the Oxford University Museum of Natural History, and the people of Wales have long asked for the Red Lady to come home. The cave can be reached only at low tide, over rough rocks, and the tide comes in fast, so visitors must check the tide times with great care. From the cliff path above, part of the Wales Coast Path, you can look down on one of the oldest places of human honour in the world."],
  ["Ogof ym muriau calchfaen arfordir deheuol Gŵyr, rhwng Porth Einon a Rhosili, yw Twll yr Afr ym Mhaviland. Heddiw mae’r tonnau’n cyrraedd ei cheg ar y llanw. Pan ddaeth pobl yma gyntaf, roedd y môr ymhell i ffwrdd, ac edrychai’r ogof dros wastadedd eang lle crwydrai mamothiaid, rhinoserosod blewog, ceirw llychlyn a cheffylau gwyllt.",
   "## Y Ddynes Goch",
   "Ym mis Ionawr 1823 cloddiodd y Parchedig William Buckland, athro daeareg yn Rhydychen, yn yr ogof a chanfod sgerbwd wedi’i liwio’n goch tywyll ag ocr, wedi’i osod gyda gwiail a modrwyau o ifori mamoth a chregyn gwichiaid. Oherwydd yr addurniadau tybiodd mai menyw oedd hi, a’i bod yn perthyn i oes y Rhufeiniaid. Glynodd yr enw: Dynes Goch Paviland.",
   "## Dyn ifanc, 33,000 o flynyddoedd yn ôl",
   "Mae gwyddoniaeth heddiw’n adrodd stori lawer hŷn. Esgyrn dyn ifanc ydynt, yn ei ugeiniau efallai, ac mae dyddio radiocarbon yn dangos iddo gael ei gladdu tua 33,000 o flynyddoedd yn ôl, yn Hen Oes y Cerrig, cyn i’r llenni iâ mawr olaf orchuddio Cymru. Fe’i gosodwyd yn yr ogof yn ofalus, ei gorff wedi’i liwio ag ocr coch, gydag addurniadau a phenglog mamoth gerllaw.",
   "Mae hynny’n gwneud Paviland y gladdedigaeth ddefodol hynaf y gwyddom amdani ym Mhrydain, ac un o’r hynaf yng ngorllewin Ewrop. Mae miloedd o offer fflint a gafwyd yn yr ogof yn dangos i bobl ddychwelyd ati dro ar ôl tro dros filoedd o flynyddoedd, a’i pharchu efallai fel lle arbennig.",
   "## Ymweld",
   "Cedwir yr esgyrn yn Amgueddfa Hanes Natur Prifysgol Rhydychen, ac mae pobl Cymru wedi gofyn ers tro i’r Ddynes Goch gael dod adref. Dim ond ar drai y gellir cyrraedd yr ogof, dros greigiau garw, ac mae’r llanw’n dod i mewn yn gyflym, felly rhaid i ymwelwyr wirio amserau’r llanw’n ofalus iawn. O’r llwybr ar y clogwyn uwchben, rhan o Lwybr Arfordir Cymru, gallwch edrych i lawr ar un o’r mannau hynaf yn y byd lle bu pobl yn anrhydeddu eu meirw."]),

 "stdavids": (
  ["St Davids, Tyddewi, stands near the western tip of Pembrokeshire, where Wales reaches out into the Atlantic. In Welsh it was first called Mynyw, in Latin Menevia. It is the smallest city in Britain, and for fifteen hundred years it has been the holiest place in Wales.",
   "## Dewi’s monastery",
   "In the 6th century Dewi, the patron saint of Wales, founded his monastery here in Glyn Rhosyn, the Vale of Roses, a hollow in the land hidden from raiders at sea. His monks lived by a strict rule, ploughing the fields by hand and drinking only water. Close by, on the cliffs, is the chapel and holy well of his mother, Non, where tradition holds that he was born in a storm.",
   "## A centre of learning",
   "St Davids became the chief church of Wales and a great house of learning. Asser, who went from here to serve King Alfred and wrote his life, was a scholar of St Davids. So was Bishop Sulien, and his son Rhygyfarch, who wrote the Life of Saint David around 1090. Viking raiders attacked it many times, and in 999 they killed Bishop Morgeneu, but the church rose again each time.",
   "## Two pilgrimages to equal Rome",
   "Kings came here: William the Conqueror in 1081, and Henry II in 1171. Around 1123 Pope Callixtus II declared that two pilgrimages to St Davids were worth one to Rome, and three were worth one to Jerusalem, and pilgrims walked the roads of Wales to Dewi’s shrine for centuries.",
   "The cathedral that stands today was begun in 1181 by Bishop Peter de Leia. The Lord Rhys, prince of Deheubarth, was buried here in 1197, and so was Edmund Tudur, father of Harri Tudur. Beside it are the great ruins of the Bishop’s Palace, built by Bishop Henry de Gower in the 14th century, with its arcades of chequered stone.",
   "On 1 March, Dydd Gŵyl Dewi, St Davids is the heart of the national day of Wales."],
  ["Saif Tyddewi ger pen gorllewinol Sir Benfro, lle mae Cymru’n ymestyn allan i’r Iwerydd. Mynyw oedd ei henw Cymraeg cyntaf, Menevia yn Lladin. Hi yw’r ddinas leiaf ym Mhrydain, ac am bymtheg can mlynedd hi fu lle mwyaf sanctaidd Cymru.",
   "## Mynachlog Dewi",
   "Yn y chweched ganrif sefydlodd Dewi, nawddsant Cymru, ei fynachlog yma yng Nglyn Rhosyn, pant yn y tir o’r golwg i ysbeilwyr ar y môr. Roedd ei fynachod yn byw dan reol lem, yn aredig y caeau â llaw ac yn yfed dŵr yn unig. Gerllaw, ar y clogwyni, mae capel a ffynnon sanctaidd ei fam, Non, lle mae traddodiad yn dweud iddo gael ei eni mewn storm.",
   "## Canolfan dysg",
   "Daeth Tyddewi’n brif eglwys Cymru ac yn dŷ dysg mawr. Ysgolhaig o Dyddewi oedd Asser, a aeth oddi yma i wasanaethu’r Brenin Alfred ac ysgrifennu ei fuchedd. Felly hefyd yr Esgob Sulien, a’i fab Rhygyfarch, a ysgrifennodd Fuchedd Dewi tua 1090. Ymosododd ysbeilwyr Llychlynnaidd arni lawer gwaith, ac yn 999 lladdasant yr Esgob Morgeneu, ond cododd yr eglwys eto bob tro.",
   "## Dwy bererindod gystal ag un i Rufain",
   "Daeth brenhinoedd yma: Gwilym Goncwerwr yn 1081, a Harri II yn 1171. Tua 1123 cyhoeddodd y Pab Calixtus II fod dwy bererindod i Dyddewi’n gyfwerth ag un i Rufain, a thair ag un i Gaersalem, a cherddodd pererinion ffyrdd Cymru at gysegrfa Dewi am ganrifoedd.",
   "Dechreuwyd yr eglwys gadeiriol sy’n sefyll heddiw yn 1181 gan yr Esgob Peter de Leia. Claddwyd yr Arglwydd Rhys, tywysog Deheubarth, yma yn 1197, a hefyd Edmwnd Tudur, tad Harri Tudur. Wrth ei hochr mae adfeilion mawr Llys yr Esgob, a godwyd gan yr Esgob Henry de Gower yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar ddeg, gyda’i fwâu o gerrig brith.",
   "Ar 1 Mawrth, Dydd Gŵyl Dewi, Tyddewi yw calon diwrnod cenedlaethol Cymru."]),

 "dale": (
  ["Mill Bay is a small, steep-sided cove on the Dale peninsula, at the mouth of Milford Haven in Pembrokeshire. It is quiet today, but on the evening of 7 August 1485 it was where the history of Britain turned, for here Harri Tudur came home to Wales to claim a crown.",
   "## The son of prophecy",
   "Harri was born at Pembroke Castle in 1457, a grandson of Owain Tudur of Penmynydd on Ynys Môn. For fourteen years he had lived in exile in Brittany and France. All that time the Welsh bards had been singing of y mab darogan, the son of prophecy, who would come to win back the crown of Britain for the Welsh, and many of them sang that Harri was the one.",
   "## The landing",
   "He sailed from Harfleur in Normandy with a small army of French and Breton soldiers and English and Welsh exiles, perhaps two or three thousand men. They landed at Mill Bay, and it is told that Harri knelt on the shore and said the psalm Judica me, Deus: ‘Judge me, O God, and plead my cause.’",
   "There is also a story of Rhys ap Thomas, the most powerful man in south-west Wales, who had sworn that Harri would enter Wales only over his body. It is said that he kept his word by lying under Mullock Bridge, near Dale, while Harri’s army marched over it, and then rode to join him.",
   "## The march to Bosworth",
   "From Dale the army marched through Haverfordwest and north along the coast to Cardigan and Machynlleth, then east through Welshpool into England. All along the way the Welsh came to join him. Rhys ap Thomas brought a large force of men from the south. On 22 August 1485 Harri won the battle of Bosworth under the banner of the red dragon, and the grandson of a family from Môn became King Henry VII."],
  ["Cilfach fach â llethrau serth ar benrhyn Dale, wrth geg Aberdaugleddau yn Sir Benfro, yw Mill Bay. Mae’n dawel heddiw, ond ar noson 7 Awst 1485 dyma’r fan lle trodd hanes Prydain, oherwydd yma y daeth Harri Tudur adref i Gymru i hawlio coron.",
   "## Y mab darogan",
   "Ganwyd Harri yng Nghastell Penfro yn 1457, yn ŵyr i Owain Tudur o Benmynydd ar Ynys Môn. Am bedair blynedd ar ddeg bu’n byw’n alltud yn Llydaw ac yn Ffrainc. Yr holl amser hwnnw bu beirdd Cymru’n canu am y mab darogan, a fyddai’n dod i ennill coron Prydain yn ôl i’r Cymry, a chanodd llawer ohonynt mai Harri oedd hwnnw.",
   "## Y glaniad",
   "Hwyliodd o Harfleur yn Normandi gyda byddin fach o filwyr Ffrengig a Llydewig ac alltudion o Loegr a Chymru, rhyw ddwy neu dair mil o wŷr efallai. Glaniasant ym Mill Bay, a dywedir i Harri benlinio ar y lan a dweud y salm Judica me, Deus: ‘Barn fi, O Dduw, a dadlau fy nadl.’",
   "Mae stori hefyd am Rys ap Thomas, gŵr mwyaf grymus de-orllewin Cymru, a oedd wedi tyngu na ddeuai Harri i Gymru ond dros ei gorff. Dywedir iddo gadw ei air drwy orwedd dan Bont Mullock, ger Dale, tra martsiai byddin Harri drosti, ac yna marchogaeth i ymuno ag ef.",
   "## Y daith i Bosworth",
   "O Dale martsiodd y fyddin drwy Hwlffordd ac i’r gogledd ar hyd yr arfordir i Aberteifi a Machynlleth, yna i’r dwyrain drwy’r Trallwng i Loegr. Ar hyd y ffordd daeth y Cymry i ymuno ag ef. Daeth Rhys ap Thomas â llu mawr o wŷr o’r de. Ar 22 Awst 1485 enillodd Harri frwydr Bosworth dan faner y ddraig goch, a daeth ŵyr i deulu o Fôn yn Frenin Harri VII."]),

 "fishguard": (
  ["Carregwastad Point is a rocky headland on the north coast of Pembrokeshire, a few miles west of Fishguard, near the village of Llanwnda. It is a wild and lonely place, and it is where the last invasion of Britain began.",
   "## The landing, 22 February 1797",
   "Britain and France were at war. The French planned to land an army in Britain to stir up a rising, and a force called the Légion Noire, the Black Legion, about 1,400 men under the American-born Colonel William Tate, set sail in four warships. They were meant to land near Bristol, but the winds carried them to Pembrokeshire. On the evening of 22 February they anchored off Carregwastad and climbed the cliffs in the dark, hauling up their guns and powder.",
   "They made their headquarters at the farmhouse of Trehowel nearby and spread out over the countryside.",
   "## Pembrokeshire stands up",
   "The people of the county did not wait. Lord Cawdor gathered the Pembroke Yeomanry, the Fencibles, the militia and sailors from the ships in Milford Haven, and hundreds of local men and women came out to join them with pitchforks, scythes and old guns. The Fishguard shoemaker Jemima Nicholas, it is told, captured twelve Frenchmen with her pitchfork. Tradition says the women of the district in their red cloaks and tall black hats looked from a distance like a regiment of soldiers.",
   "## The surrender",
   "Within two days the invaders had had enough. Tate sent word that he would surrender, the terms were agreed at the Royal Oak inn in Fishguard, and on 24 February 1797 the whole French force laid down its arms on Goodwick Sands. The Pembroke Yeomanry was given ‘Fishguard’ as a battle honour, the only one ever won by a British regiment on British soil.",
   "A memorial stone was set up on the headland at Carregwastad in 1897, a hundred years later. Today the Wales Coast Path passes it, and in Fishguard Town Hall you can see the Last Invasion Tapestry, made by the people of the town in 1997."],
  ["Pentir creigiog ar arfordir gogleddol Sir Benfro, ychydig filltiroedd i’r gorllewin o Abergwaun, ger pentref Llanwnda, yw Pen Carregwastad. Mae’n lle gwyllt ac unig, a dyma lle dechreuodd y goresgyniad olaf ar Brydain.",
   "## Y glaniad, 22 Chwefror 1797",
   "Roedd Prydain a Ffrainc yn rhyfela. Cynlluniodd y Ffrancod i lanio byddin ym Mhrydain i godi gwrthryfel, a hwyliodd llu o’r enw y Légion Noire, y Lleng Ddu, tua 1,400 o wŷr dan y Cyrnol William Tate a aned yn America, mewn pedair llong ryfel. Ger Bryste oedd y bwriad, ond cariodd y gwyntoedd hwy i Sir Benfro. Ar noson 22 Chwefror angorasant ger Carregwastad a dringo’r clogwyni yn y tywyllwch, gan lusgo eu gynnau a’u powdr i fyny.",
   "Gwnaethant eu pencadlys yn ffermdy Trehywel gerllaw a lledaenu dros y wlad.",
   "## Sir Benfro’n codi",
   "Ni arhosodd pobl y sir. Casglodd yr Arglwydd Cawdor Iwmyn Penfro, y milwyr lleol a morwyr o’r llongau yn Aberdaugleddau, a daeth cannoedd o ddynion a merched lleol allan i ymuno â nhw gyda phicweirch, pladuriau a hen ynnau. Meddir i’r grydd o Abergwaun, Jemima Nicholas, ddal deuddeg o Ffrancod â’i phicwarch. Mae traddodiad yn dweud bod gwragedd yr ardal yn eu clogynnau coch a’u hetiau duon tal yn edrych o bell fel catrawd o filwyr.",
   "## Yr ildio",
   "O fewn deuddydd roedd y goresgynwyr wedi cael digon. Anfonodd Tate air y byddai’n ildio, cytunwyd ar yr amodau yng Ngwesty’r Royal Oak yn Abergwaun, ac ar 24 Chwefror 1797 gosododd y llu Ffrengig cyfan ei arfau i lawr ar Draeth Wdig. Rhoddwyd ‘Fishguard’ yn anrhydedd brwydr i Iwmyn Penfro, yr unig un a enillwyd erioed gan gatrawd Brydeinig ar dir Prydain.",
   "Codwyd carreg goffa ar y pentir yng Ngharregwastad yn 1897, gan mlynedd yn ddiweddarach. Heddiw mae Llwybr Arfordir Cymru’n mynd heibio iddi, ac yn Neuadd y Dref Abergwaun gallwch weld Tapestri’r Goresgyniad Olaf, a wnaed gan bobl y dref yn 1997."]),

 "dinorwig": (
  ["The slate landscape of north-west Wales stretches from the quarries of Penrhyn at Bethesda and Dinorwig at Llanberis to the great mines of Blaenau Ffestiniog and the valleys of Nantlle and Dyffryn Ogwen. In 2021 it was named a World Heritage Site by UNESCO: the Slate Landscape of Northwest Wales.",
   "## The quarry on the mountain",
   "Dinorwig quarry climbs the side of Elidir Fawr above Llyn Padarn, terrace above terrace, like a staircase cut into the mountain. It was worked from the late 18th century by the Assheton Smith family, and at its height it employed around 3,000 men, making it one of the largest slate quarries in the world. Penrhyn quarry at Bethesda was just as great.",
   "## Roofing the world",
   "Welsh slate is hard, fine and lasts for centuries. In the 19th century the quarrymen of Gwynedd split it into roofing slates by the million. Little railways carried them down to Port Dinorwic at Y Felinheli, Port Penrhyn at Bangor and Porthmadog, and ships took them to roof the terraces of Britain’s cities, and houses and halls from America to Australia. It was said that Wales roofed the world.",
   "## A Welsh way of life",
   "The slate villages were deeply Welsh. In the caban, the hut where they ate their midday meal, the quarrymen held debates, eisteddfodau and readings, and many were poets, singers and scholars. The chapels, choirs and brass bands of the slate country were famous across Wales. In 1900 the men of Penrhyn began a stand for their rights that lasted three years, one of the longest disputes in the history of British industry.",
   "Dinorwig quarry closed in 1969. Its workshops at Gilfach Ddu are now the National Slate Museum, where you can see the great water wheel and watch slate being split by hand. Deep inside the mountain is the Dinorwig power station, Electric Mountain."],
  ["Mae tirwedd llechi gogledd-orllewin Cymru’n ymestyn o chwareli’r Penrhyn ym Methesda a Dinorwig yn Llanberis i fwyngloddiau mawr Blaenau Ffestiniog a dyffrynnoedd Nantlle ac Ogwen. Yn 2021 fe’i henwyd yn Safle Treftadaeth y Byd gan UNESCO: Tirwedd Llechi Gogledd-orllewin Cymru.",
   "## Y chwarel ar y mynydd",
   "Mae chwarel Dinorwig yn dringo ochr Elidir Fawr uwchben Llyn Padarn, ponc uwchben ponc, fel grisiau wedi’u torri yn y mynydd. Fe’i gweithiwyd o ddiwedd y ddeunawfed ganrif gan deulu Assheton Smith, ac ar ei hanterth cyflogai tua 3,000 o ddynion, gan ei gwneud yn un o chwareli llechi mwyaf y byd. Roedd chwarel y Penrhyn ym Methesda yr un mor fawr.",
   "## Toi’r byd",
   "Mae llechen Cymru’n galed, yn fain ac yn para am ganrifoedd. Yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg holltai chwarelwyr Gwynedd hi’n llechi to wrth y miliwn. Cariai rheilffyrdd bach hwy i lawr i’r Felinheli, Porth Penrhyn ym Mangor a Phorthmadog, ac âi llongau â hwy i doi rhesi tai dinasoedd Prydain, a thai a neuaddau o America i Awstralia. Dywedid mai Cymru a roes do ar y byd.",
   "## Ffordd Gymreig o fyw",
   "Roedd pentrefi’r llechi’n Gymreig i’r carn. Yn y caban, lle bwytaent eu cinio, cynhaliai’r chwarelwyr ddadleuon, eisteddfodau a darlleniadau, ac roedd llawer ohonynt yn feirdd, yn gantorion ac yn ysgolheigion. Roedd capeli, corau a bandiau pres ardaloedd y llechi’n enwog drwy Gymru. Yn 1900 dechreuodd gwŷr y Penrhyn safiad dros eu hawliau a barodd dair blynedd, un o’r anghydfodau hiraf yn hanes diwydiant Prydain.",
   "Caeodd chwarel Dinorwig yn 1969. Mae ei gweithdai yng Ngilfach Ddu bellach yn Amgueddfa Lechi Cymru, lle gallwch weld yr olwyn ddŵr fawr a gwylio hollti llechi â llaw. Yn ddwfn y tu mewn i’r mynydd mae gorsaf bŵer Dinorwig, y Mynydd Trydan."]),

 "senedd": (
  ["The Senedd building stands on the waterfront of Cardiff Bay, where ships once loaded the coal of the valleys for the ports of the world. It is the home of Senedd Cymru, the Welsh Parliament, and it was opened by the Queen on St David’s Day, 1 March 2006.",
   "## A parliament won back",
   "For centuries Wales had no parliament of its own. Owain Glyndŵr held one at Machynlleth in 1404, and campaigners from Cymru Fydd to Megan Lloyd George and Gwynfor Evans kept the hope alive. In September 1997 the people of Wales voted yes to devolution, and the National Assembly for Wales first met in 1999. It met at first in temporary rooms while its permanent home was built.",
   "## Slate, oak and glass",
   "The building was designed by Richard Rogers. It is made of Welsh materials: a great plinth of Welsh slate stepping down to the water, and a roof of Welsh oak that sweeps over the glass walls. At its centre a funnel of oak rises from the roof and reaches down into the debating chamber, the Siambr, bringing daylight and fresh air into the room where the members meet. The building gathers rainwater and draws heat from the earth beneath it.",
   "Its walls are of glass because a parliament should be open, and visitors can walk in, sit in the public gallery and watch the members of the Senedd at work, in Welsh and in English.",
   "## Growing powers",
   "In 2011 the people voted for the Assembly to make its own laws. In 2020 it took the name Senedd Cymru, the Welsh Parliament, and in 2021 sixteen and seventeen year olds voted in its election for the first time. From 2026 it has 96 members. Beside it stands the red-brick Pierhead building of 1897, and together they look out over the bay where the old docks once stood."],
  ["Saif adeilad y Senedd ar lan y dŵr ym Mae Caerdydd, lle bu llongau unwaith yn llwytho glo’r cymoedd i borthladdoedd y byd. Dyma gartref Senedd Cymru, ac fe’i hagorwyd gan y Frenhines ar Ddydd Gŵyl Dewi, 1 Mawrth 2006.",
   "## Ennill senedd yn ôl",
   "Am ganrifoedd nid oedd gan Gymru ei senedd ei hun. Cynhaliodd Owain Glyndŵr un ym Machynlleth yn 1404, a chadwodd ymgyrchwyr o Gymru Fydd i Megan Lloyd George a Gwynfor Evans y gobaith yn fyw. Ym mis Medi 1997 pleidleisiodd pobl Cymru o blaid datganoli, a chyfarfu Cynulliad Cenedlaethol Cymru gyntaf yn 1999. Cyfarfu ar y dechrau mewn ystafelloedd dros dro tra codid ei gartref parhaol.",
   "## Llechen, derw a gwydr",
   "Cynlluniwyd yr adeilad gan Richard Rogers. Fe’i gwnaed o ddeunyddiau Cymreig: sylfaen fawr o lechi Cymru’n camu i lawr at y dŵr, a tho o dderw Cymru’n ysgubo dros y muriau gwydr. Yn ei ganol mae twndis o dderw’n codi o’r to ac yn ymestyn i lawr i’r Siambr, gan ddod â golau dydd ac awyr iach i’r ystafell lle mae’r aelodau’n cyfarfod. Mae’r adeilad yn casglu dŵr glaw ac yn tynnu gwres o’r ddaear oddi tano.",
   "Mae ei furiau o wydr am y dylai senedd fod yn agored, a gall ymwelwyr gerdded i mewn, eistedd yn yr oriel gyhoeddus a gwylio aelodau’r Senedd wrth eu gwaith, yn Gymraeg ac yn Saesneg.",
   "## Pwerau’n tyfu",
   "Yn 2011 pleidleisiodd y bobl i’r Cynulliad lunio ei gyfreithiau ei hun. Yn 2020 cymerodd yr enw Senedd Cymru, ac yn 2021 pleidleisiodd pobl un ar bymtheg a dwy ar bymtheg oed yn ei hetholiad am y tro cyntaf. O 2026 mae ganddi 96 o aelodau. Wrth ei hochr saif adeilad brics coch y Pierhead o 1897, a gyda’i gilydd maent yn edrych dros y bae lle bu’r hen ddociau."]),

 "blaenavon": (
  ["Blaenavon, Blaenafon, lies high at the head of the Afon Lwyd valley in Torfaen, on the north-eastern edge of the south Wales coalfield. Its hillsides hold everything that made the industrial revolution: iron ore, coal and limestone, side by side. In 2000 the whole Blaenavon Industrial Landscape was named a World Heritage Site by UNESCO.",
   "## The ironworks",
   "In 1789 three partners, Thomas Hill, Thomas Hopkins and Benjamin Pratt, built an ironworks here with three blast furnaces driven by steam. Within a few years it was one of the largest ironworks in the world. Its furnaces, the cast house and the great water balance tower, which lifted loaded trams by the weight of water, still stand, among the best preserved of their age anywhere. Next to them is Stack Square, where the skilled workers lived.",
   "## Big Pit",
   "Coal for the furnaces came from the hills around. Big Pit was sunk in the 1860s and worked until 1980. Today it is Big Pit National Coal Museum, where former miners take visitors down the shaft, 90 metres underground, to see the roads, the stables of the pit ponies and the coal face where the men of the valleys worked.",
   "## Steel for the world",
   "In 1878 two cousins, Sidney Gilchrist Thomas and Percy Gilchrist, the chemist at the Blaenavon works, found at Blaenavon how to take the phosphorus out of iron when making steel. Their discovery meant that the phosphoric iron ores of Europe and America could be made into good steel, and steel production across the world was transformed.",
   "## A proud town",
   "The people of Blaenavon built their own chapels and schools, and in 1895 the Workmen’s Hall, paid for from the miners’ own wages, with its library and reading room. St Peter’s Church was built by the ironmasters in 1804. Blaenavon, the town that helped to make the modern world, is one of the finest places in Wales to see how the valleys lived and worked."],
  ["Mae Blaenafon yn uchel ym mhen cwm Afon Lwyd yn Nhorfaen, ar ymyl gogledd-ddwyreiniol maes glo’r de. Ar ei llethrau mae popeth a wnaeth y chwyldro diwydiannol: mwyn haearn, glo a chalchfaen, ochr yn ochr. Yn 2000 enwyd Tirwedd Ddiwydiannol Blaenafon gyfan yn Safle Treftadaeth y Byd gan UNESCO.",
   "## Y gwaith haearn",
   "Yn 1789 cododd tri phartner, Thomas Hill, Thomas Hopkins a Benjamin Pratt, waith haearn yma gyda thair ffwrnais chwyth wedi’u gyrru gan stêm. O fewn ychydig flynyddoedd roedd yn un o weithfeydd haearn mwyaf y byd. Mae ei ffwrneisi, y tŷ castio a’r tŵr cydbwysedd dŵr mawr, a godai dramiau llawn â phwysau dŵr, yn sefyll o hyd, ymhlith y gorau o’u hoes yn unman. Wrth eu hymyl mae Sgwâr y Stac, lle’r oedd y gweithwyr crefftus yn byw.",
   "## Pwll Mawr",
   "Daeth glo i’r ffwrneisi o’r bryniau o amgylch. Suddwyd y Pwll Mawr yn y 1860au a’i weithio hyd 1980. Heddiw mae’n Amgueddfa Lofaol Genedlaethol Cymru, lle mae cyn-lowyr yn mynd ag ymwelwyr i lawr y siafft, 90 metr dan ddaear, i weld y ffyrdd, stablau’r merlod pwll a’r ffas lo lle gweithiai dynion y cymoedd.",
   "## Dur i’r byd",
   "Yn 1878 darganfu dau gefnder, Sidney Gilchrist Thomas a Percy Gilchrist, cemegydd gwaith Blaenafon, ym Mlaenafon sut i dynnu’r ffosfforws allan o haearn wrth wneud dur. Golygai eu darganfyddiad y gellid troi mwynau haearn ffosfforig Ewrop ac America’n ddur da, a gweddnewidiwyd cynhyrchu dur ledled y byd.",
   "## Tref falch",
   "Cododd pobl Blaenafon eu capeli a’u hysgolion eu hunain, ac yn 1895 Neuadd y Gweithwyr, a dalwyd o gyflogau’r glowyr eu hunain, gyda’i llyfrgell a’i hystafell ddarllen. Codwyd Eglwys Sant Pedr gan y meistri haearn yn 1804. Mae Blaenafon, y dref a helpodd i greu’r byd modern, yn un o’r mannau gorau yng Nghymru i weld sut roedd y cymoedd yn byw ac yn gweithio."]),
}
